From 987d5ac8213c52194aa9496296e23c5ee319cbcf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 01:03:07 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] generic-parallel interfaces: arcane backend

Backend of the behaviours, the models and the material properties based on
the accelerator API of Arccore, the core component of the Arcane framework:

- `ArcaneBackendExecutionPolicy` holds a queue of Arccore, which is
  required. Its identifier depends on the accelerator mode of Arccore
  (`arcane:cuda`, `arcane:hip`, `arcane:host`), so that a calling code
  compiled by a C++ compiler and a library compiled by a CUDA compiler
  agree on it;
- the queue selects the device at runtime: a library compiled for CUDA
  computes on the GPU with a CUDA queue, on the threads of the host with a
  `Thread` queue, sequentially with a `Sequential` queue. The memory space
  is `device`. A null queue, and a CUDA queue with a library not compiled
  by a CUDA compiler, are refused;
- the commands are launched asynchronously (`RunQueue::ScopedAsync`, the
  state of the queue is restored), then the queue is synchronized for a
  synchronous computation. The batches are split in commands of at most
  2^30 points, the kernels of Arccore computing the index of a thread on
  32 bits. If a launch fails, the commands already launched are completed
  before the exception is propagated;
- the statistics are updated by native atomic operations (those of Arccore
  do not support unsigned 64 bits integers). Without statistics given by
  the calling code, they are held by the unified memory of Arccore, as the
  copies of the shared data;
- the stack size of the threads of the GPU is increased with a probe
  kernel, as with RAJA;
- `arcane` configuration: Arccore built with CUDA as shared libraries,
  found in `Arccore_ROOT` (its configuration file enables the CUDA
  language in the calling project), sources of the backend compiled by
  clang as a CUDA compiler, sources of the `generic` interface by clang as
  a C++ compiler. The device functions of CUDA are excluded from the
  overloads of the host compilation (`-Xarch_host
  -fgpu-exclude-wrong-side-overloads`): the code of the behaviours is also
  executed on the host, where an unqualified call to `max` (`using
  namespace std`) would select the device function of CUDA;
- tests of the behaviours (CUDA, `Thread` and `Sequential` queues, queues
  of the execution policies, TDLS) and of the material properties,
  documentation.
